A note on what this does and does not cover.

Copyright protects the EXPRESSION of an idea — this code, these words, this
artwork — and it does so automatically from the moment they are written. That
protection is real and this notice makes the claim explicit.

Copyright does not protect the idea itself. Anyone may build a neighbourhood
noticeboard; what they may not do is take this implementation of one. Claiming
otherwise would not be enforceable, and a notice that overreaches is easier to
dismiss than one that is accurate.

If the concept itself needs protecting, that is a matter for a patent attorney
or for a written agreement with specific people, not for a licence file.

On ownership: copyright vests in the author automatically, from the moment the
work is written. It is held here by a person because that is who wrote it. When
a company exists to hold it instead, the transfer is a written assignment — and
naming a company that does not yet exist would be a weaker claim than this one,
not a stronger one.
